<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Hui of Chu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 488–432 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Hui of Chu (reigned 488–432 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Chu who restored its power after the Wu invasion of 506 BCE. He survived a revolt by his kinsman Bai Gong Sheng in 479 BCE and in the same year annexed the State of Chen, later also absorbing Cai. A bell he gave to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-yi-of-zeng">Marquis Yi of Zeng</a> was found in the marquis's tomb, buried around 433 BCE.</p></div>
